    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 all 36 tests pass, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the E.164 national-number length validation in `TelephoneE164.java` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/`).\n\n**Change:** After the existing \"8\u201315 digits total\" check, the normalizer now enforces an exact national-number length for recognised country codes:\n- `+61` \u2192 exactly 9 national digits\n- `+1` \u2192 exactly 10 national digits\n\nA wrong length returns `null`, which `ValidateOwnerFields` already turns into a 400 with `telephone` in the errors array. Unrecognised country codes (e.g. `+64`) keep only the general 8\u201315 rule, so existing behaviour is preserved.\n\n**Design notes:**\n- The two recognised codes (`61`, `1`) don't overlap as prefixes (`61\u2026` never starts with `1`), so prefix matching against a `Map` is unambiguous regardless of iteration order \u2014 I documented this in the code.\n- Verified against the acceptance tests: `+61 123` (3 national digits) \u2192 400 (Cp26); `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` (9 national digits) \u2192 accepted (Cp02/Cp08); `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 accepted via the general rule (Cp08); and the shared `uniqueTelephone()` helper (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits) stays valid across all other tests.",
